1
00:00:00,375 --> 00:00:03,300
Imagine arriving at a
farm to repair an engine.

2
00:00:03,325 --> 00:00:04,575
The doors are closed.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,375
The family does not answer.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,288
You hear cattle, and a
dog, but no human voice.

5
00:00:09,312 --> 00:00:11,212
You wait, then get on with your work.

6
00:00:11,238 --> 00:00:12,400
Hours pass.

7
00:00:12,425 --> 00:00:15,388
When you leave, you still
have not seen a single person.

8
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:19,100
Later that afternoon, neighbors
discover six people dead inside the

9
00:00:19,125 --> 00:00:20,062
property.

10
00:00:20,088 --> 00:00:23,675
This happened at Hinterkaifeck,
in Bavaria, in April of nineteen

11
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:24,625
twenty-two.

12
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:28,352
And it leaves a question more
unsettling than an empty house.

13
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,418
Was somebody still there
while the mechanic worked?

14
00:00:31,443 --> 00:00:35,080
Reports of activity after the
killings have made this case famous.

15
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:37,755
But the evidence is
not a camera recording.

16
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,880
It is a collection of observations,
statements, and later recollections

17
00:00:41,905 --> 00:00:43,968
. Some details are strong.

18
00:00:43,992 --> 00:00:46,755
Others have become sharper in the
telling than they ever were in the

19
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:47,742
record.

20
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:51,468
This is the story of six lives,
a farm that seemed to keep

21
00:00:51,492 --> 00:00:55,080
functioning, and an investigation
that never established who was

22
00:00:55,105 --> 00:00:56,433
responsible.

23
00:00:56,935 --> 00:01:00,548
The images you see are
illustrative reconstructions, not

24
00:01:00,572 --> 00:01:02,725
photographs of the crime scene.

25
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:07,285
Hinterkaifeck was a working farm near
the small settlement of Groebern,

26
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:08,485
north of Munich.

27
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:12,222
It was apart from its neighbors, but
not beyond their sight or hearing.

28
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:16,335
Calling it a house lost in an endless
forest creates the wrong picture.

29
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,660
Fields and paths connected
it to ordinary village life.

30
00:01:19,685 --> 00:01:23,285
The older couple were Andreas
Gruber, sixty-three, and his wife,

31
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:25,398
Cazilia, seventy-two.

32
00:01:25,422 --> 00:01:28,735
Their daughter, Viktoria
Gabriel, was thirty-five.

33
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:32,838
Her husband, Karl, had been reported
killed in the First World War.

34
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:37,390
Viktoria had two children:
seven-year-old Cazilia, often

35
00:01:37,415 --> 00:01:40,065
called Cilli, and two-year-old Josef.

36
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:43,340
On March thirty-first,
a new employee arrived.

37
00:01:43,365 --> 00:01:45,890
Maria Baumgartner was forty-five.

38
00:01:45,915 --> 00:01:49,090
Her sister accompanied her
to the farm and then left.

39
00:01:49,115 --> 00:01:51,302
Maria had come to begin a job.

40
00:01:51,328 --> 00:01:53,727
She would not survive
her first night there.

41
00:01:53,752 --> 00:01:57,968
These people are often reduced to a
list at the beginning of a mystery.

42
00:01:58,445 --> 00:02:01,882
But their daily lives explain
why absences would eventually

43
00:02:01,908 --> 00:02:06,045
matter: a child missing school,
a family missing worship

44
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:08,732
, work left without an owner to answer.

45
00:02:08,758 --> 00:02:11,158
Six different lives were interrupted.

46
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:14,758
The youngest two could not possibly
have understood the adult conflicts

47
00:02:14,782 --> 00:02:17,385
later used to explain their deaths.

48
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:21,832
Before the murders, there were
reports of disturbing incidents.

49
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:25,407
Neighbors said Andreas had described
a damaged lock and tracks in the

50
00:02:25,432 --> 00:02:29,232
snow leading toward the farm,
without a matching trail away.

51
00:02:29,257 --> 00:02:32,770
Other accounts mention an unfamiliar
newspaper and noises in the roof

52
00:02:32,795 --> 00:02:36,970
space. A former servant was said to
have believed the place was haunted.

53
00:02:36,995 --> 00:02:40,820
It is easy to combine these into
a perfect sequence: a stranger

54
00:02:40,845 --> 00:02:43,907
arrives, hides upstairs, and waits.

55
00:02:43,932 --> 00:02:48,122
The surviving material does not
let us watch that sequence happen.

56
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:51,887
The research chronology distinguishes
contemporary statements from

57
00:02:51,912 --> 00:02:54,200
stories recorded years afterward.

58
00:02:54,225 --> 00:02:57,887
Some descriptions of the attic
noises, for example, survive through

59
00:02:57,912 --> 00:03:01,412
secondhand accounts given long
after nineteen twenty-two.

60
00:03:01,437 --> 00:03:05,137
Even a genuine track in snow
cannot identify a person,

61
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:09,250
establish how long they stayed,
or explain another route out

62
00:03:09,275 --> 00:03:12,012
. A noise has many possible causes.

63
00:03:12,037 --> 00:03:14,475
That does not make the witnesses liars.

64
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:17,652
It means the uncertainty
belongs in the story.

65
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:21,530
Andreas appears to have been
concerned about interference at his

66
00:03:21,555 --> 00:03:22,592
property.

67
00:03:22,617 --> 00:03:26,767
Whether all the famous warning signs
came from the same intruder remains

68
00:03:26,792 --> 00:03:27,892
unknown.

69
00:03:27,917 --> 00:03:31,980
The attic becomes frightening
precisely because we cannot say who

70
00:03:32,005 --> 00:03:34,345
, if anyone, occupied it.

71
00:03:35,305 --> 00:03:38,517
The killings are generally placed
on the evening of Friday, March

72
00:03:38,542 --> 00:03:39,667
thirty-first.

73
00:03:39,692 --> 00:03:43,255
Investigators reconstructed an
attack in the barn area and attacks

74
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,080
inside the living quarters.

75
00:03:45,105 --> 00:03:48,655
Andreas, his wife, Viktoria,
and Cilli were found in the

76
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:51,580
barn. Maria was found in her room.

77
00:03:51,605 --> 00:03:54,667
Josef was found in his
carriage inside the house.

78
00:03:54,692 --> 00:03:57,305
All six had suffered fatal violence.

79
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:00,357
We do not need to show it
to understand the loss.

80
00:04:00,885 --> 00:04:04,447
A commonly repeated reconstruction
has members of the family drawn into

81
00:04:04,472 --> 00:04:06,235
the barn one after another.

82
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:08,860
But no survivor described that sequence.

83
00:04:08,885 --> 00:04:12,560
The precise order, the reason they
entered, and the number of attackers

84
00:04:12,585 --> 00:04:14,860
were not established by an eyewitness.

85
00:04:14,885 --> 00:04:18,797
The connected buildings mattered:
the barn, stable, and living rooms

86
00:04:18,822 --> 00:04:20,810
formed a route through the property.

87
00:04:20,835 --> 00:04:24,572
Someone could move between
agricultural spaces and the house.

88
00:04:24,597 --> 00:04:27,972
That is a fact about the building,
not proof that its layout was

89
00:04:27,997 --> 00:04:29,737
familiar to the killer.

90
00:04:30,265 --> 00:04:33,440
As the night ended, the
farm remained standing.

91
00:04:33,465 --> 00:04:36,952
There was no obvious disaster
visible from the road.

92
00:04:36,977 --> 00:04:40,552
To someone passing outside,
the difference between a quiet

93
00:04:40,577 --> 00:04:44,480
household and a catastrophe
could be impossible to see.

94
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:49,278
On Saturday, visitors came to the
property and received no answer.

95
00:04:49,302 --> 00:04:51,115
Cilli did not appear at school.

96
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:54,078
By Sunday, the family
was absent from church.

97
00:04:54,102 --> 00:04:57,790
Each observation was troubling, but
each could also seem explainable in

98
00:04:57,815 --> 00:04:58,978
isolation.

99
00:04:59,002 --> 00:05:02,878
A working farm was not a place where
every closed door immediately meant

100
00:05:02,902 --> 00:05:04,065
an emergency.

101
00:05:04,090 --> 00:05:07,002
There were animals to
tend and errands to run.

102
00:05:07,028 --> 00:05:10,702
People could assume that somebody
else had spoken to the household.

103
00:05:10,727 --> 00:05:13,718
On Monday, the postman made his delivery.

104
00:05:14,270 --> 00:05:17,882
A later statement recalled a detail
at the kitchen window: the little

105
00:05:17,907 --> 00:05:20,820
carriage he usually noticed
was no longer there.

106
00:05:20,845 --> 00:05:24,645
It is a haunting memory, but it
was recorded decades afterward.

107
00:05:24,670 --> 00:05:27,958
Its date matters when deciding
how much weight to give it.

108
00:05:27,982 --> 00:05:32,145
Other reported signs, including
smoke and activity around the farm,

109
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:35,945
helped create the impression
that ordinary life continued.

110
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:39,608
None provides a continuous
account of what happened inside.

111
00:05:39,632 --> 00:05:41,572
That gap is essential.

112
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:46,275
We know the bodies were not discovered
until Tuesday, April fourth.

113
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:49,988
We do not know, hour by hour, who
entered the property between the

114
00:05:50,012 --> 00:05:51,962
murders and that discovery.

115
00:05:51,987 --> 00:05:56,062
The delay gave the case its
terrible shape: visible buildings,

116
00:05:56,088 --> 00:06:00,165
audible animals, and no one who
could answer for the people within.

117
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:04,562
Tuesday brought one of the
case's most unsettling witnesses.

118
00:06:04,588 --> 00:06:08,738
Albert Hofner arrived to repair
a small engine used on the farm.

119
00:06:08,762 --> 00:06:11,775
According to the account preserved
in the research chronology, he

120
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,725
arrived in the morning and
could not find the residents.

121
00:06:14,750 --> 00:06:17,562
He looked around, heard
animals, and waited.

122
00:06:17,588 --> 00:06:21,075
Then he worked in the attached
engine shed for several hours.

123
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:25,175
He sang or whistled as he worked,
and tested the engine afterward

124
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,877
. Still, no resident came out.

125
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:32,392
His account also described the
dog and the state of a barn door.

126
00:06:32,417 --> 00:06:36,568
Details appeared to have changed
during his visit: he later saw the dog

127
00:06:36,592 --> 00:06:40,142
tied outside, and noticed
a barn entrance open.

128
00:06:40,167 --> 00:06:44,318
The exact comparison depends on his
observations and on statements from

129
00:06:44,342 --> 00:06:46,355
the people who arrived later.

130
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:50,380
If these descriptions are accurate,
they suggest another person had

131
00:06:50,405 --> 00:06:53,282
access to the property during that period.

132
00:06:53,810 --> 00:06:57,735
They do not tell us whether that
person was the murderer, an accomplice,

133
00:06:57,760 --> 00:07:00,872
or somebody whose presence
was never explained.

134
00:07:00,898 --> 00:07:03,372
Imagine the limit of Hofner's view.

135
00:07:03,398 --> 00:07:07,210
A shed wall, an engine,
the sound of his own tools.

136
00:07:07,235 --> 00:07:10,610
He could be close to the center
of the mystery without seeing it.

137
00:07:10,635 --> 00:07:14,748
He eventually left and reported
that the repair was done, but nobody

138
00:07:14,772 --> 00:07:16,150
had answered him.

139
00:07:17,085 --> 00:07:19,460
Concern finally became a search.

140
00:07:19,485 --> 00:07:23,085
Neighbor Lorenz Schlittenbauer went
to Hinterkaifeck with Michael Poell

141
00:07:23,110 --> 00:07:24,535
and Jakob Sigl.

142
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:28,560
On April fourth, the men entered the
agricultural part of the property

143
00:07:28,585 --> 00:07:30,348
and found four victims.

144
00:07:30,372 --> 00:07:32,660
The others were found inside the house.

145
00:07:32,685 --> 00:07:34,472
The police were notified.

146
00:07:34,498 --> 00:07:38,072
Before investigators could preserve
the scene, however, people had

147
00:07:38,097 --> 00:07:39,685
already moved through it.

148
00:07:39,710 --> 00:07:42,287
Schlittenbauer moved some of the bodies.

149
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:46,328
His explanation was that he was
looking for little Josef, whom he had

150
00:07:46,352 --> 00:07:50,478
acknowledged as his son, and thought
the child might still be saved.

151
00:07:50,502 --> 00:07:53,653
Other visitors and onlookers
added to the disturbance.

152
00:07:53,678 --> 00:07:56,828
These actions would later become
part of the suspicion surrounding

153
00:07:56,852 --> 00:07:57,978
Schlittenbauer.

154
00:07:58,002 --> 00:08:01,478
They also created a practical
problem independent of anybody's

155
00:08:01,502 --> 00:08:05,315
guilt: the first documented
scene was already an altered

156
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:06,252
scene.

157
00:08:06,278 --> 00:08:10,365
Investigators had to ask the discoverers
to explain and reconstruct what

158
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:12,242
they had originally seen.

159
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:16,595
A footprint, an open door, or an
object out of place could have more

160
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:17,932
than one author.

161
00:08:17,958 --> 00:08:21,245
In a modern investigation,
access would be controlled

162
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:22,470
immediately.

163
00:08:22,495 --> 00:08:26,432
Here, the urgent human response
and the need to preserve evidence

164
00:08:26,458 --> 00:08:27,508
collided.

165
00:08:27,532 --> 00:08:30,345
The case had not simply lost four days.

166
00:08:30,370 --> 00:08:33,920
It had lost some of the distinctions
between what the attacker left and

167
00:08:33,945 --> 00:08:35,960
what everyone else changed.

168
00:08:36,895 --> 00:08:39,770
The physical evidence did
not resolve the mystery.

169
00:08:39,795 --> 00:08:42,407
Money and valuables
remained at the property.

170
00:08:42,432 --> 00:08:46,108
That complicated a simple robbery
explanation, although it could not

171
00:08:46,132 --> 00:08:48,308
prove that nothing had been taken.

172
00:08:48,332 --> 00:08:52,282
Leaving some money behind does not
reveal a killer's entire motive.

173
00:08:52,307 --> 00:08:54,982
Nor was the weapon
immediately established.

174
00:08:55,007 --> 00:08:58,308
During the dismantling of the
farm the following year, a heavy

175
00:08:58,332 --> 00:09:01,795
agricultural tool was found
hidden in the roof space.

176
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:05,257
It was a root-cutting hoe,
often described in English as a

177
00:09:05,282 --> 00:09:06,322
mattock.

178
00:09:06,875 --> 00:09:10,525
A surviving account of the prosecutor's
report described its unusual

179
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:13,537
construction, including
a projecting screw.

180
00:09:13,562 --> 00:09:16,450
Investigators connected
the tool to the injuries.

181
00:09:16,475 --> 00:09:19,812
The farm tool mattered because it
came from the property and because it

182
00:09:19,838 --> 00:09:21,200
had been concealed.

183
00:09:21,225 --> 00:09:23,800
But it did not arrive
with a name attached.

184
00:09:23,825 --> 00:09:27,012
Someone familiar with the farm
might know where to find it.

185
00:09:27,037 --> 00:09:30,450
A stranger could also find a
tool in a working agricultural

186
00:09:30,475 --> 00:09:31,362
building.

187
00:09:31,387 --> 00:09:35,550
Every object invited a story,
and more than one story could fit

188
00:09:35,575 --> 00:09:39,655
. This is why a plausible
reconstruction is not the same

189
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:41,692
thing as a solved case.

190
00:09:41,718 --> 00:09:44,442
The tool narrowed part of the mechanism.

191
00:09:44,468 --> 00:09:47,530
The remaining valuables
complicated motive.

192
00:09:47,555 --> 00:09:51,330
Neither established the identity of
the person who used violence that

193
00:09:51,355 --> 00:09:52,245
night.

194
00:09:53,205 --> 00:09:56,655
Return to the question that makes
Hinterkaifeck so disturbing.

195
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:59,605
Did the killer remain on
the farm after the murders?

196
00:09:59,630 --> 00:10:03,405
The suggestion comes from several
strands: reports of smoke,

197
00:10:03,430 --> 00:10:07,392
indications interpreted as food
preparation, the condition of the

198
00:10:07,417 --> 00:10:10,142
animals, and the mechanic's observations.

199
00:10:10,167 --> 00:10:14,005
Taken together, they make
continued human activity a serious

200
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:15,230
possibility.

201
00:10:15,255 --> 00:10:17,482
But each strand has limits.

202
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:21,635
The research chronology explicitly
cautions that the extent of feeding

203
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:25,085
and milking between the killings
and discovery is uncertain.

204
00:10:25,110 --> 00:10:28,072
Smoke cannot tell you
whose hand tended a fire.

205
00:10:28,097 --> 00:10:31,735
A meal's remains cannot, by
themselves, give every event a

206
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:33,235
reliable time.

207
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:36,848
And a dog seen in different places
points toward movement, not an

208
00:10:36,872 --> 00:10:38,510
identified person.

209
00:10:38,535 --> 00:10:40,410
One person might have stayed.

210
00:10:40,435 --> 00:10:42,060
Someone might have returned.

211
00:10:42,085 --> 00:10:44,862
Some observations might be incomplete.

212
00:10:45,390 --> 00:10:48,965
These possibilities do not carry
equal proof simply because we can

213
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:50,190
imagine them.

214
00:10:50,215 --> 00:10:54,002
What we can say is narrower: the
reports are difficult to reconcile with

215
00:10:54,027 --> 00:10:57,540
a completely observed,
sealed, empty property.

216
00:10:57,565 --> 00:11:01,390
What we cannot say is that a murderer
certainly spent four uninterrupted

217
00:11:01,415 --> 00:11:04,202
days comfortably living
beside the victims.

218
00:11:04,228 --> 00:11:08,315
That familiar claim turns an
inference into an eyewitness account

219
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:11,752
. The real mystery is already
powerful enough without making the

220
00:11:11,777 --> 00:11:13,930
evidence say more than it does.

221
00:11:14,890 --> 00:11:18,090
Suspicion repeatedly returned to
people with connections to the

222
00:11:18,115 --> 00:11:19,290
household.

223
00:11:19,315 --> 00:11:22,965
Lorenz Schlittenbauer had a relationship
with Viktoria, and there had

224
00:11:22,990 --> 00:11:26,915
been conflict over Josef's
paternity and financial support.

225
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:30,402
His conduct during the
discovery also drew attention.

226
00:11:30,427 --> 00:11:33,540
Those are reasons
investigators examined him.

227
00:11:33,565 --> 00:11:34,990
They are not a verdict.

228
00:11:35,015 --> 00:11:38,915
He was never convicted of these
murders, and this film does not identify

229
00:11:38,940 --> 00:11:40,467
him as the killer.

230
00:11:40,995 --> 00:11:44,520
Another disturbing part of the
family history is documented:

231
00:11:44,545 --> 00:11:48,232
Andreas and Viktoria had been
prosecuted for incest in nineteen

232
00:11:48,257 --> 00:11:49,270
fifteen.

233
00:11:49,295 --> 00:11:52,970
When discussing this, it is important
to remember the unequal power

234
00:11:52,995 --> 00:11:56,857
between a father and daughter, and
to avoid turning Viktoria's history

235
00:11:56,882 --> 00:11:58,832
into an excuse for her murder.

236
00:11:58,857 --> 00:12:02,732
The record does not establish that
this history caused the killings.

237
00:12:02,757 --> 00:12:06,757
Other suggestions involved
outsiders or people seeking money.

238
00:12:06,782 --> 00:12:10,197
A stranger might account
for unfamiliar traces.

239
00:12:10,675 --> 00:12:13,800
A familiar person might account
for knowledge of the buildings.

240
00:12:13,825 --> 00:12:17,375
Yet neither category explains
every uncertain detail.

241
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:20,887
If a theory can absorb any evidence,
it can become impossible to

242
00:12:20,912 --> 00:12:21,812
test.

243
00:12:21,837 --> 00:12:25,950
The useful question is not which
suspect makes the most dramatic ending.

244
00:12:25,975 --> 00:12:29,562
It is which claim can be supported
independently, which claim

245
00:12:29,587 --> 00:12:33,337
contradicts the record, and
what remains unexplained.

246
00:12:33,362 --> 00:12:36,400
Hinterkaifeck offers
suspicion in abundance.

247
00:12:36,425 --> 00:12:39,687
It does not offer the proof needed
to turn a named person into the

248
00:12:39,712 --> 00:12:40,790
answer.

249
00:12:41,775 --> 00:12:45,887
One theory imagines Viktoria's
husband, Karl Gabriel, secretly

250
00:12:45,912 --> 00:12:48,925
surviving the war and
returning to take revenge.

251
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:52,412
It has the shape of a thriller:
a missing soldier, a hidden

252
00:12:52,437 --> 00:12:54,962
identity, a violent homecoming.

253
00:12:54,987 --> 00:12:58,362
The case researchers point
to a less cinematic record.

254
00:12:58,387 --> 00:13:02,412
Authorities investigated Karl's
death, received confirmation of his

255
00:13:02,437 --> 00:13:06,537
burial in France, and had
testimony from former comrades.

256
00:13:06,562 --> 00:13:10,752
That does not identify the person
responsible at Hinterkaifeck.

257
00:13:11,255 --> 00:13:14,805
It does mean the returning-husband
story should not be presented as

258
00:13:14,830 --> 00:13:16,342
equally supported.

259
00:13:16,367 --> 00:13:20,142
The same caution applies to
supernatural explanations.

260
00:13:20,167 --> 00:13:24,092
A frightened servant and noises
above a ceiling do not establish a

261
00:13:24,117 --> 00:13:25,117
haunting.

262
00:13:25,142 --> 00:13:29,192
A ghost story can connect every gap
because it does not have to leave

263
00:13:29,217 --> 00:13:30,267
evidence.

264
00:13:30,292 --> 00:13:34,457
A historical investigation has
to live with the gaps instead.

265
00:13:34,985 --> 00:13:39,035
Over the years, repeated retellings
have also made precise details seem

266
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:43,097
settled: exactly who walked
into the barn first, exactly how

267
00:13:43,122 --> 00:13:46,447
long an intruder waited,
exactly what they ate.

268
00:13:46,472 --> 00:13:48,972
Much of that precision is reconstruction.

269
00:13:48,997 --> 00:13:52,810
Even a source can contain an
error, or a remembered detail

270
00:13:52,835 --> 00:13:54,722
recorded decades late.

271
00:13:54,747 --> 00:13:57,997
Our responsibility is to
keep those limits visible.

272
00:13:58,022 --> 00:14:02,135
Rejecting a dramatic explanation
does not solve Hinterkaifeck

273
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:06,327
. It helps preserve the difference
between a real tragedy and

274
00:14:06,352 --> 00:14:08,905
a story that has borrowed its names.

275
00:14:09,865 --> 00:14:12,740
The buildings were demolished
in nineteen twenty-three.

276
00:14:12,765 --> 00:14:15,777
The people who could have
explained what happened are gone.

277
00:14:15,802 --> 00:14:19,765
Modern methods cannot recover an
intact scene that was never preserved

278
00:14:19,790 --> 00:14:23,902
for them. Even a future answer to
a family relationship would not

279
00:14:23,927 --> 00:14:26,527
automatically identify a murderer.

280
00:14:26,552 --> 00:14:28,902
The case remains unresolved.

281
00:14:28,927 --> 00:14:31,502
So where does that leave
the opening question?

282
00:14:31,527 --> 00:14:35,427
Hofner repaired an engine at a farm
whose residents could no longer

283
00:14:35,452 --> 00:14:36,692
answer him.

284
00:14:37,195 --> 00:14:40,895
His account, and the other reports
of activity, suggest that somebody

285
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:43,582
may have moved through the
property after the killings.

286
00:14:43,607 --> 00:14:47,545
The identity, timing, and purpose
of that person remain uncertain.

287
00:14:47,570 --> 00:14:50,770
We should resist filling those
empty spaces with certainty simply

288
00:14:50,795 --> 00:14:53,095
because certainty feels like an ending.

289
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:56,845
Hinterkaifeck is not only a puzzle
about who might have hidden upstairs.

290
00:14:56,870 --> 00:15:00,957
It is also the story of
Andreas, Cazilia, Viktoria,

291
00:15:00,982 --> 00:15:03,057
Cilli, Josef, and Maria.

292
00:15:03,082 --> 00:15:05,597
A child who should have
returned to school.

293
00:15:06,150 --> 00:15:08,137
A worker who had just arrived.

294
00:15:08,162 --> 00:15:11,300
A family whose silence
was understood too late.

295
00:15:11,325 --> 00:15:12,937
The farm is gone.

296
00:15:12,962 --> 00:15:14,575
The question remains.

297
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:18,250
But remembering what the evidence
cannot tell us is part of remembering

298
00:15:18,275 --> 00:15:20,065
these people honestly.
